	"<h3><strong>The Brass Fortresses, Volume 2</strong><br></h3>
	<strong><em>Author:Lack-Of-Grace</em></strong><br><br>
	
	I remember quite fondly of the daes in which I celebrated my lyfe as a scholar along the upper most levels of Dytikos Pyrgos. We feasted during the dae and bloated our bodies with wine during the nite. At the home of functionary Brass-of-Faith, we bathed together and drank together in the name of the Allfather. It was to rejoice—rejoice that we lived. Bah, no matter! I have watched several Tennite celebrations and they are, strange. They eat a lot, and drink more wine than us. More exotic wines at that. They are bountiful as they are beautiful.<br><br>

	On the first day in which I was to catalogue and survey this foreign land, I was invited to a feast, as I mentioned above. It was a fine feast, served with duck “lathered” in lettuce, cooked and boiled in such a way that the latent juices soaked the duck to add a certain flavor to the meal. Of course, Duck had not been seen in the lower levels in yills, not since the Gentry had driven a majority of the species to extinction, but I digress.<br><br>


	On the first day, I recall vividly a grand, dwarven hall, not unlike those found above floor 600 and above. There was a mighty column that denoted what was to be cooked, brewed, and/or fried. It was a list, a list of glorious meals to be served for lunch and dinner in fact, there was no breakfast for most of the Hammerhold nobility subsisted off of porridges laced with aged cheeses and meats. While I wrote that, I had neglected to mention that each food item that had been inscribed onto the brass column were meals the last king had consumed; supposedly it was an act of veneration, if you will; the nuances of such are, of course, lost to me.<br><br>


	Eventually, I did inquire with the Seneschal, and the list was exhausting. 36 liters of wheat flour and barley, four hundred deer, four hundred rabbits or cabbits (it didn’t seem to matter), several hundred cows (I was told roughly 200), 600 small birds of varying types… 66 units of garlic, 3 units of raisins and at least 5,000 drams of wine at the very least, made from exotic Otavan breweries and ancient aelven techniques. Truly, it was quite the blinding display. I neglected to mention too that this was merely one daes food, or so I was told. I assume this is merely apocryphal; it is impossible to reign over such opulence and not fall to the Inhumane of B.<br><br>


	The banquet that was presented before me (and that menu) had lacked a master of the table, I noticed. I mentioned it to the Seneschal, who had spoken to me in such a kindly way. He politely explained that it was his duty, and indeed it was. When I finally had settled down at the dining hall the following day, I noticed the lack of rigid table manners for they ate without discipline or an ounce of enjoyment. It was enjoyment for enjoyment's sake and a waste of food at that.<br><br>


	As we ate during that fateful feast, I started to inquire about the dietary habits of the occasional levyman. The contents of which were unassuming, to say. Fatty meats and hard meads; it contrasted the traditional ration issued to levymen of the floors. The Itrion was made of roughly two hundred sesame seeds (if that) and honey. It was a fine dish issued to levymen who needed that quick boost of muster in their lyfe and a meal of the ages. A lot of the taste had come from the honey and some of the sugar thrown in.<br><br>


	I vaguely recall the recipe as I was taught during my younger yills. First, as I said, you ought to have thrown the seeds ‘pon the heating dish of your choosing and let the seeds turn to a fine brown. It is imperative to note that one must watch the seeds to ensure they don’t turn to a vile sludge. After a good fifteen motes of this, the seeds had to be baked in such a way that the aroma of cooked sesame seeds could be smelled. It was only after then that one would need to add honey to the dish only after caramelizing your aforementioned honey. The point of itrion was that it was to be made as a snack, nothing more, nothing less. Coffee is, of course, a rarity, reserved only for Gentrymen ‘pon the uppermost layers; it should be clarified.<br><br>


	I mumbled too much about the meal our men ate back home, I think. The feast had eventually concluded and I gorged myself ‘pon a fine lettuce-lathered duck, which was consumed with my zilphium sauce, which I had personally requested be ground up and turned to a fine sludge with trace amounts of sugar and melted cheese. The meal had no doubt garnered great interest in the royalty, who were upset that such a herb was impossible to find in the lands around Hammerhold and instead only found in the Rust Lands, but I digress.<br><br>


	I rested ‘pon my bed and slept for the night without a crumb of disturbance or anything else of that dreadful sort. I have never been a man to enjoy an opulent celebration, save for the tyme when I had rejoiced in the academy but that was another tyme. The next month and a half was one of traveling and exploration. I learned much from the dwarven artificers in Hammerhold and ate a great many foods as well as I no doubt implied earlier. Of the great sights and beauty I saw, I had witnessed a fountain of molten gold upheld by arcyne means and some other sort of arcyne arts. I stood there in amazement at such verbose architecture and took note for later. It was a heavy contrast from the cogworks of the upper levels and the stages that were erected into the walls, stages that had depicted historic events throughout our history as a people, I ought to add.<br><br>


	The month-long expedition had gone without much fanfare or quite honestly, anything of note. There was beauty as far as the eye could see, and food to be enjoyed. At one point in my journey, though, I had a fateful encounter with a Psydonite. The Psydonite, a kinsman, if you will, was a hostile sort. They demanded to know why I had defiled their holy symbol as they called it, so we spoke in private. We debated a great moment of faith and what it means to be made in the Allfather's image. He was happy to hear new kinsmen have arrived, wielding ancient and powerful weapons so they might combat the forces of the arch enemy. Granted, he resented me for my own being and spoke greatly of Otava, my next journey. <br><br>


	I had neglected to mention that I was to be accompanied by two "adjudicators," as they called them in Otava. They were no different than the Brass or Steam Knights of the upper levels, but they were nothing compared to them for while we wore bronze, they wore silver and wielded harsh steel. They were led by an “Inspector” of some sort. Anyway, after talking with the kinsmen, we departed for Otava under the pretense of me being a diplomatic envoy which I was anything but. <br><br>"
